WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.198 position:63%
There's an old Martin Gardner column where

00:00:02.198 --> 00:00:04.551 position:63%
he describes an alien visiting Earth who asks

00:00:04.551 --> 00:00:06.852 position:63%
a professor about the best artifact to bring

00:00:06.852 --> 00:00:09.000 position:63%
back to understand all of human knowledge.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:10.700 position:63%
The professor offers up an

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.450 position:63%
encyclopedia with its many volumes.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:15.050 position:63%
The alien says, no no no, this cannot work,

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.650 position:63%
because his ship is incredibly weight constrained,

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:20.227 position:63%
and adding even a nanogram would compromise

00:00:20.227 --> 00:00:22.349 position:63%
his chances of a successful journey home.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:23.599 position:63%
But he pulls out a thin,

00:00:23.849 --> 00:00:25.400 position:63%
one meter rod that's part of the ship.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.747 position:63%
and says he should be able to record all

00:00:27.747 --> 00:00:29.978 position:63%
the encyclopedia's contents by making a

00:00:29.978 --> 00:00:32.210 position:63%
single well positioned mark on that rod.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.010 position:63%
The Professor, astounded,

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:35.710 position:63%
asks how this could be possible.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:37.810 position:63%
"Very simple," the alien says.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.964 position:63%
"Your encyclopedia has fewer than a

00:00:39.964 --> 00:00:42.060 position:63%
hundred distinct letters and other symbols,

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.087 position:63%
so let's associate each symbol

00:00:44.087 --> 00:00:45.610 position:63%
with a unique pair of digits.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.516 position:63%
Now we can turn each letter of your encyclopedia

00:00:48.516 --> 00:00:50.459 position:63%
into the corresponding pair of digits.

00:00:50.612 --> 00:00:52.767 position:63%
Stack all of those together and put a

00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:54.299 position:63%
zero point in front of it,

00:00:54.299 --> 00:00:56.512 position:63%
and we can interpret the result as one

00:00:56.512 --> 00:00:58.724 position:63%
incredibly precise real number between

00:00:58.724 --> 00:00:59.462 position:63%
zero and one.

00:01:00.012 --> 00:01:02.056 position:63%
What I'll do is carefully make a mark

00:01:02.056 --> 00:01:04.262 position:63%
at exactly this proportion along the rod,

00:01:04.712 --> 00:01:05.412 position:63%
and when I get home,

00:01:05.612 --> 00:01:07.177 position:63%
my fellow scientists can carefully

00:01:07.177 --> 00:01:08.162 position:63%
measure that position,

00:01:08.662 --> 00:01:10.131 position:63%
and dividing its distance to the

00:01:10.131 --> 00:01:11.512 position:63%
start by the length of the rod,

00:01:11.762 --> 00:01:13.562 position:63%
written out with all these digits of precision,

00:01:14.012 --> 00:01:16.112 position:63%
that will let us recover your full encyclopedia.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.558 position:63%
Okay, you don't need me to tell you

00:01:19.558 --> 00:01:21.210 position:63%
why this anecdote is so absurd.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:23.195 position:63%
It takes only about five letters

00:01:23.195 --> 00:01:24.780 position:63%
before the precision required is

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.509 position:63%
already down to the size of an atom.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.252 position:63%
And yet, if you modify this story to

00:01:29.252 --> 00:01:30.960 position:63%
something that seems more reasonable,

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:32.634 position:63%
say where the professor is allowed

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:34.009 position:63%
to give a hard drive to that alien,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.275 position:63%
if you were to ask what is the most

00:01:36.275 --> 00:01:38.040 position:63%
efficient way to encode all of the

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.310 position:63%
encyclopedia's contents onto that hard drive,

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:41.933 position:63%
how do you compress it down

00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:43.210 position:63%
to as few bits as possible?

00:01:43.412 --> 00:01:45.092 position:63%
The aliens' idea with the rod is a

00:01:45.092 --> 00:01:47.012 position:63%
lot more relevant than you might expect.

00:01:47.362 --> 00:01:49.312 position:63%
One of the best compression algorithms we have,

00:01:49.462 --> 00:01:50.812 position:63%
known as an arithmetic coding,

00:01:51.112 --> 00:01:53.623 position:63%
works basically by associating arbitrarily

00:01:53.623 --> 00:01:55.608 position:63%
long sequences of text with very,

00:01:55.608 --> 00:01:58.412 position:63%
very specific real numbers between zero and one.

00:01:58.662 --> 00:02:00.462 position:63%
It might sound absurd, but it's very beautiful.

00:02:00.862 --> 00:02:02.112 position:63%
And if you want to learn how it works,

00:02:02.412 --> 00:02:03.353 position:63%
whenever you're in the mood

00:02:03.353 --> 00:02:04.362 position:63%
to hop out of the shorts feed,

00:02:04.612 --> 00:02:07.244 position:63%
take a look at the third video I did in a trilogy

00:02:07.244 --> 00:02:09.562 position:63%
under the title Compression is Intelligence.

